    ("Which early 1960s MIT/GE/Bell Labs time-sharing OS's complexity inspired Ken Thompson to build simpler Unix?", "Multics", ["CTSS", "TOPS-10", "CP/M"], "Even the name 'Unix' began as a pun on Multics ('Uniplexed' vs 'Multiplexed')."),
    ("Which 1970s microprocessor by MOS Technology powered the Apple II, Commodore 64 (6510 variant), BBC Micro, and NES?", "MOS 6502", ["Zilog Z80", "Motorola 68000", "Intel 8080"], "Designed by Chuck Peddle's team at a fraction of rival CPU prices."),
    ("Which 16/32-bit Motorola processor powered the original 1984 Macintosh, Commodore Amiga, Atari ST, and Sega Genesis?", "Motorola 68000", ["Intel 80286", "MOS 6502", "ARM1"], "Renowned for its clean orthogonal 32-bit instruction set."),
    ("Which RISC microprocessor family born at Acorn Computers in Cambridge in 1985 now powers nearly all smartphones?", "ARM", ["MIPS", "SPARC", "PowerPC"], "Co-designed by Sophie Wilson and Steve Furber for low power efficiency."),
    ("Which open-standard RISC instruction set architecture originated at UC Berkeley in 2010 without licensing royalties?", "RISC-V", ["OpenSPARC", "ARMv8", "Alpha"], "Modular base integer ISA plus optional extensions for vectors, atomics, and floats."),
    ("Which pioneering 1963 graphical thesis system by Ivan Sutherland introduced interactive constraint drawing and CAD?", "Sketchpad", ["LightPen", "NLS", "Smalltalk-72"], "Demonstrated object instantiation and constraint solving on MIT's TX-2 computer."),
    ("Which 1974–1977 Bell Labs text-processing pair by Aho, Weinberger, Kernighan, and Lesk revolutionized pattern scanning?", "awk (and lex/yacc)", ["sed and ed", "grep and m4", "perl and tcl"], "AWK derives its name from its three authors' surname initials."),
    ("Which landmark 1973–1981 Xerox PARC personal workstation introduced Ethernet, laser printing, WYSIWYG editing, and bitmapped displays?", "Xerox Alto", ["IBM 5100", "DEC PDP-11", "MIT Lisp Machine"], "Inspired Steve Jobs's famous 1979 visit to PARC."),
    ("Which programming paradigm emphasizes immutable values, pure mathematical functions, and higher-order combinators?", "Functional programming", ["Procedural programming", "Imperative assembly", "Aspect-oriented programming"], "Traces roots directly to Alonzo Church's lambda calculus."),
    ("What software design pattern wraps a shared expensive or remote object to control access, caching, or lazy loading?", "Proxy pattern", ["Flyweight pattern", "Observer pattern", "Singleton pattern"], "Distinguished from Decorator by controlling access rather than merely extending behavior."),
    ("What behavioral design pattern lets an object notify multiple subscribed dependents automatically on state changes?", "Observer pattern", ["Strategy pattern", "Visitor pattern", "Factory pattern"], "Underpins reactive UI frameworks and event buses."),
    ("In video game engines, what architectural pattern decouples entity identity from component data arrays for cache-friendly iteration?", "ECS (Entity-Component-System)", ["Deep inheritance tree", "Model-View-Controller", "Active Record"], "Improves CPU L1/L2 cache locality across thousands of active game objects."),
    ("In multiplayer game networking, what technique advances local player movement immediately before server confirmation arrives?", "Client-side prediction (with reconciliation)", ["Lockstep determinism", "Packet pacing", "Interpolation only"], "Eliminates sluggish input feel over 50–150 ms internet round-trips."),
    ("In lockstep RTS networking (like classic Age of Empires or StarCraft), why must all clients run identically given identical inputs?", "Floating-point determinism across clients", ["Zero client CPU usage", "Dynamic packet loss skipping", "Server-rendered video frames"], "Even a tiny rounding divergence causes desync across thousands of units."),
    ("In audio signal processing, what algorithm computes an N-point Discrete Fourier Transform in O(N log N) operations?", "FFT (Fast Fourier Transform)", ["Wavelet Haar filter", "FIR convolution", "Laplace transform"], "Modern form popularized by James Cooley and John Tukey in 1965."),
    ("Which pioneering 1982 sci-fi cyberpunk novel by William Gibson coined the term 'cyberspace'?", "Neuromancer", ["Snow Crash", "Do Androids Dream of Electric Sheep?", "The Diamond Age"], "Followed console cowboy Case jacking into the Matrix."),
    ("Which 1992 cyberpunk novel by Neal Stephenson popularized the term 'Metaverse' and 'avatar'?", "Snow Crash", ["Neuromancer", "Cryptonomicon", "Ready Player One"], "Opens with Hiro Protagonist delivering pizza for the Mafia."),
    ("Which 1965 sci-fi epic novel by Frank Herbert takes place largely on the desert planet Arrakis over spice melange?", "Dune", ["Foundation", "Hyperion", "The Left Hand of Darkness"], "Adapted notably on film by Denis Villeneuve in 2021 and 2024."),
    ("Which sci-fi trilogy by Isaac Asimov follows mathematician Hari Seldon's 'psychohistory' predicting galactic empire collapse?", "Foundation", ["The Caves of Steel", "Rendezvous with Rama", "Dune"], "Originally serialized in Astounding Science-Fiction starting in 1942."),
    ("Which 1979–1984 comedic sci-fi series by Douglas Adams states the Answer to the Ultimate Question is 42?", "The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy", ["Discworld", "Red Dwarf", "Good Omens"], "Began as a BBC Radio 4 broadcast before becoming a five-book trilogy."),
    ("Which acclaimed 2008 Pixar sci-fi animated film opens with a lonely waste-collecting robot on an abandoned Earth?", "WALL-E", ["Up", "Ratatouille", "Inside Out"], "Features virtually no spoken dialogue throughout its first 35 minutes."),
    ("Which 2018 Sony Pictures Animation film pioneered comic-book halftone rendering and multi-frame animation on Miles Morales?", "Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse", ["Big Hero 6", "The Mitchells vs. the Machines", "Arcane"], "Won the Academy Award for Best Animated Feature."),
    ("Which 1999 Japanese survival-thriller novel by Koushun Takami influenced the entire modern Battle Royale genre?", "Battle Royale", ["The Hunger Games", "Lord of the Flies", "Gantz"], "Adapted into Kinji Fukasaku's 2000 cult film."),
    ("Which 1994 Super Nintendo JRPG created by Square's 'Dream Team' (Sakaguchi, Horii, Toriyama) centers on time travel?", "Chrono Trigger", ["Final Fantasy VI", "Secret of Mana", "EarthBound"], "Featured 13 distinct endings and seamless on-map battles."),
    ("Which 2011 FromSoftware action-RPG popularized 'bonfire' checkpoints, stamina combat, and interconnected Lordran level design?", "Dark Souls", ["Demon's Souls", "Bloodborne", "Sekiro"], "Spawned the 'Soulslike' genre."),
    ("Which 2017 open-world Nintendo game transformed exploration by letting players climb nearly every surface and go straight to Ganon?", "The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild", ["Tears of the Kingdom", "Ocarina of Time", "Twilight Princess"], "Won Game of the Year at The Game Awards 2017."),
    ("Which 2022 collaboration between Hidetaka Miyazaki and George R.R. Martin brought Soulsborne combat into the Lands Between?", "Elden Ring", ["Sekiro", "Armored Core VI", "Lies of P"], "Earned unanimous acclaim and over 25 million copies sold."),
    ("Which 2018 roguelike action game by Supergiant Games stars Zagreus fighting out of the Underworld?", "Hades", ["Bastion", "Dead Cells", "Binding of Isaac"], "First video game ever to win a Hugo Award for Best Video Game."),
    ("Which 2016 puzzle-platformer by Matt Makes Games (Maddy Thorson) pairs precision mountain climbing with mental-health narrative?", "Celeste", ["Hollow Knight", "Ori and the Blind Forest", "Super Meat Boy"], "Nominated for Game of the Year alongside AAA blockbusters."),
    ("Which 2017 Metroidvania by Australian indie studio Team Cherry unfolds across the subterranean insect kingdom of Hallownest?", "Hollow Knight", ["Blasphemous", "Dead Cells", "Axiom Verge"], "Hand-drawn 2D art and challenging boss fights made it an indie classic."),
    ("Which 1986 Nintendo NES side-scroller co-created by Gunpei Yokoi starred Samus Aran and helped define non-linear exploration?", "Metroid", ["Castlevania", "Mega Man", "Kid Icarus"], "Fused with Castlevania: Symphony of the Night to coin 'Metroidvania.'"),
    ("Which 1997 PlayStation milestone shifted Squaresoft from Nintendo cartridges to CD-ROMs with Aerith, Cloud, and Sephiroth?", "Final Fantasy VII", ["Final Fantasy VIII", "Xenogears", "Metal Gear Solid"], "Its pre-rendered backdrops and orchestral FMVs helped establish PlayStation worldwide."),
